just explain what exactly these changes  are going to do well the stalking  protection orders that come into force  tomorrow will enable a force like my in  Sussex Police to help a victim of  stalking almost immediately so at the  moment if you want to get some sort of  form of of injunction against the  stalker you have to apply to court  yourself and go through a very lengthy  process this will allow the police to  apply for that injunction order and it  will stop the perpetrator perhaps from  coming near you're approaching you plus  it will have some positive requirements  attached to it as well so you can  actually make sure that the perpetrator  perhaps goes to a program of help if  there's one available in your area of  course there's been some really  upsetting and troubling cases reported  about you know women who've reported  people for stalking and nothing's  happened and as a result they've gone on  to even lose their lives yeah how  important do you think yeah what the  changes are now well this is a big step  change in the right direction for all of  us unfortunately I too have been and  continued to be a victim of stalking  with a small group of individuals who  seem hell-bent on making my life really  difficult in fact one has a custodial  sentence at the moment that's been  suspended for two years so like like  many victims I really can empathize with  this and I know from my own experience  having to go through the civil courts  just to get an injunction was a terribly  gruesome experience and the civil and  the criminal courts when we originally  applied to those they let me down as  well so I think with these stalking  protection orders this gives the police  the opportunity to intervene on behalf  of the victim if you're a victim of  stalking you often don't even know and  we very often get victims who phone the  police and they say well I'm not sure so  first of all we've now invented an  acronym for fo you are so that the  police can easily identify whether it's  stalking or harassment so if the  behavior is fixated obsessive unwanted  or repeated then the police officer in  the first instance will know that that  stalking stalking  a criminal conviction attached to it now  thanks to the new law that came that's  recently come in and these stalking  protection orders are part of that new  law as well so they will allow the  police officer who's identified stalking  correctly in the first instance to put  an injunction in place to give the  victim some time and respite and then  that also and really importantly for the  victims point of view it says for the  victim I hear you I believe you and  we're going to do something about it  because very often victims by the time  they report to the police they've been  subjected to over a hundred incidents of  stalking now if one of those only  happens every week that's nearly two  years worth so that intense heightened  stative of alarm that a victim will go  through I cannot underestimate it's no  wonder they come to us with  post-traumatic stress disorder and worse  so these protection orders massively  important and we welcome in Sussex and  I'm hoping that in Sussex in my force  here tomorrow we will be one of the  first in the country to actually issue a  couple of these stalking protection  orders you know I can't help wondering  if stalking is one of those things that  we've been a bit slow as a society to  really appreciate what an impact it has  on people who are the victims of it you  were saying there but your own sort of  lived experience of what happened can  you describe to our viewers about what  it feels like to be a victim of stalking  well first you don't realize what's  happening for me because I'm a public  figure  you kind of expect a lot of noise anyway  around you you know you're not  everybody's best friend and people tend  to be quite vocal against you but this  particular individual was a relentless I  mean he was fixated he was obsessive he  emailed lots of people he went across  social media he printed stuff about me  calling me a pedophile a prostitute I  was accused of murder of child sex abuse  you name it and he then professionally  tried to attack me by contacting  professional boards that I sit on and  people I work with and it was just  relentless and there were pages and  pages across social media he was then  able to connect with others of a similar  ilk in my County so there's four of them  now  and one of them I'm in court with next  month and and this has been going on for  nearly eight years so as a you know as a  victim and I'm I'm one of the the  luckier ones because some of the victims  I meet have just had the most awful  experiences people they're their  perpetrators have been put in prison and  they know they're coming out soon and  they're terrified for their lives I had  one woman held my hands and she said  he's coming out next year Katie I know  he's going to kill me you've got to help  me  so you're absolutely right this is an  invidious crime and I think what we're  seeing is just the tip of the iceberg  because by as I said by the time a  victim comes forwards they often don't  know they're being stalked they don't  understand the difference and the  difference is really important because  that will determine what response the  police give so if the police correctly  identify stalking in the first instance  they will then go down the proper route  to gather the intelligence and build the  case file that they need to prosecute  that individual if they don't recognize  it then the victim doesn't get supported  and doesn't get help and this is why  these protection orders are so vital  they are a step in the right direction  but there is one thing police forces  need to do across the country we need to  make sure that not just the police but  all our criminal justice partners  understand that if those orders are  breached there is a consistent response  to that breach and that person is then  dealt with effectively because at the  moment as the inspection that I carried  out last year I commissioned her  Majesty's Inspectorate to do an  inspection of Sussex Police and their  response to the stalking and they made  local recommendations for us but they  made some really important ones  nationally for policing and one of the  things that they really discovered was  that the police are not doing proper  risk assessments on victims in that  first instance so we're trying a pilot  here in Sussex with Surrey and Cheshire  Police at the moment looking at a new  risk assessment for victims we found  they got one if they were in a domestic  abuse situation but a third of our  victims aren't they could be stalked by  someone who's a stranger okay.thank they  don't get that risk assessment then  thank you so much for talking so  powerfully about this important story  and also sharing your own experience  thank you as well while we've got you  and  it was reported this week that Boris  Johnson's planned to review the role of  police and crime commissioners like  myself do you think the Prime Minister's  committed to keeping you in yes  absolutely and we welcome this review  and I and my colleagues earlier this  week we had a general meeting and the  policing minister came and the Cabinet  Secretary was there and we asked them  both about the review and they said  actually they're doing a review across  all government departments and we  welcome this review because they're also  looking at potentially how they might  expand our role into the criminal  justice system so give us potentially  further responsibilities
 

